WEBVTT

00:00:01.020 --> 00:00:08.280
forest floor is alive with salamanders gathering for one night of passion we'll see unique film of

00:00:08.280 --> 00:00:13.360
the fierce battles and extraordinary mating rituals of these creatures of the night

00:00:13.360 --> 00:00:19.590
presentation of this program was made possible by the corporation for public broadcasting

00:00:19.590 --> 00:00:22.810
and by annual financial support from viewers like you

00:00:22.810 --> 00:00:24.740
music

00:00:54.250 --> 00:00:55.030
talamanders,

00:00:55.900 --> 00:01:00.000
tailed amphibians whose prehistoric ancestors crawled out of

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:09.460
primeval lakes and swamps, 100 million years before the dinosaurs arrived.

00:01:09.460 --> 00:01:15.790
According to many myths and legends, salamanders have the most remarkable powers, but the facts

00:01:15.790 --> 00:01:25.580
about them are even stranger than the fantasies.

00:01:25.580 --> 00:01:30.000
It was once believed that salamanders lived in fire, and could, when they wished to die,

00:01:30.510 --> 00:01:37.750
extinguish it. That is hardly true. But salamanders have lived through millions of years of geological

00:01:37.750 --> 00:01:59.860
change and they still flourish even today. Salamanders have a moist skin that is permeable to air and water.

00:02:00.030 --> 00:02:03.880
And that means they can only live in moist places.

00:02:03.880 --> 00:02:07.520
You might also think that it makes them very vulnerable to predators.

00:02:07.520 --> 00:02:25.850
A wolf prowling in a European forest is looking for a meal, but this fire center has an ancient

00:02:25.850 --> 00:02:30.000
and effective form of defense.

00:02:30.060 --> 00:02:35.160
black and yellow coloration is a warning of poison that is widely understood in the

00:02:35.160 --> 00:02:43.990
animal kingdom. The salamander can produce a bitter toxin from glands on its back. If

00:02:43.990 --> 00:03:00.000
you interfere with it, there can be trouble. Muscles around each gland contract.

00:03:00.380 --> 00:03:04.160
and squirt poison directly into an attacker's face.

00:03:04.160 --> 00:03:11.840
And the fire salamander escapes with its life.

00:03:11.840 --> 00:03:17.600
When the dinosaurs died out,

00:03:17.600 --> 00:03:21.380
little mammals, like today's shrews, took over the forest.

00:03:21.380 --> 00:03:24.650
Shrews are small but ferocious predators,

00:03:24.650 --> 00:03:29.480
and they need great quantities of insects and other small creatures to sustain themselves.

00:03:32.140 --> 00:03:38.070
A soft-bodied amphibian could easily be on the menu.

00:03:38.070 --> 00:03:46.290
But the marbled salamander's vivid coloration again warns of danger.

00:03:46.290 --> 00:03:53.770
Hungry shrew recklessly takes no notice.

00:03:53.770 --> 00:03:57.720
Poisonous slime is released from glands above the tail,

00:03:57.720 --> 00:04:00.000
which the salamander lashes vigorously,

00:04:00.160 --> 00:04:05.480
so attracting the shrew's attention to its least vulnerable and most distasteful part.

00:04:05.480 --> 00:04:18.570
The shrew persists, but eventually the taste of the salamander is altogether too nasty.

00:04:18.570 --> 00:04:30.000
Garter snakes?

00:04:30.060 --> 00:04:36.710
hunt through the drier parts of north american forests but salamanders here have a special way

00:04:36.710 --> 00:04:54.640
of foiling them this salamander has no lungs it breathes through its skin so it can't suffocate

00:04:54.640 --> 00:05:00.000
its glands produce a slime which sticks around the snake's jaws this

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:09.260
This slows down the attack until the snake, which does have a lung, simply runs out of breath and is forced to let go.

00:05:09.260 --> 00:05:27.080
With leaves and soil stuck around its head, the snake can't work its jaws properly and it'll be several days before it can go hunting again.

00:05:31.120 --> 00:05:37.490
the appalachian mountains of north america salamander heaven

00:05:37.490 --> 00:05:42.890
billions of them belonging to over 20 different species live here

00:05:42.890 --> 00:05:46.510
most ancient of them all is a giant with an ancestry

00:05:46.510 --> 00:05:52.450
that stretches back 200 million years it lives

00:05:52.450 --> 00:05:55.500
in the streams it's as long as a man's arm

00:05:55.500 --> 00:05:59.920
and it lurks beautifully camouflaged among the rocks of the riverbeds

00:06:01.280 --> 00:06:14.900
it's the hellbender. Flaps of skin increase its surface area, and so enable it to absorb

00:06:14.900 --> 00:06:25.650
all the oxygen it needs directly from the water. Hellbenders, the original salamanders,

00:06:25.650 --> 00:06:30.000
still live permanently in water. But 100 million

00:06:30.000 --> 00:06:37.870
years ago many of their relations moved up onto land. Some Appalachian salamanders

00:06:37.870 --> 00:06:43.220
still have to return to water to breed but others have become totally terrestrial.

00:06:43.220 --> 00:06:49.330
Redback salamanders are more abundant than any other back-boned animal in

00:06:49.330 --> 00:06:54.240
North America. There are two and a half million of them in every square kilometer

00:06:54.240 --> 00:07:00.000
of Appalachian forest. Their eyesight is excellent and they notice the slightest movement.

00:07:00.540 --> 00:07:04.000
but they've got to get close to their prey if they're going to catch it.

00:07:04.000 --> 00:07:18.630
A seal salamander is also out hunting.

00:07:18.630 --> 00:07:24.960
With excellent night vision, it too must wait for its prey to come within range.

00:07:24.960 --> 00:07:30.000
The redback suddenly becomes aware of danger,

00:07:30.000 --> 00:07:33.980
The seal salamander keeps still and isn't noticed.

00:07:33.980 --> 00:07:38.930
And then something extraordinary happens.

00:07:38.930 --> 00:07:46.110
The red back amputates the end of its tail.

00:07:46.110 --> 00:07:47.710
It's a clean break.

00:07:47.710 --> 00:07:53.810
The vertebrae have separated neatly and automatic muscle contractions wave the discarded end,

00:07:53.810 --> 00:07:56.210
diverting the seal salamander's attention.

00:08:02.400 --> 00:08:09.530
the seal salamander gets a snack and the red back escapes with its life the wound closes up straight

00:08:09.530 --> 00:08:17.980
away there's no loss of blood and the lost tip will soon grow back so in the end everybody wins

00:08:17.980 --> 00:08:30.000
something costa rica a land of volcanoes mountains covered in thick foam

00:08:30.000 --> 00:08:36.800
and enveloped in almost permanent fog, ideal conditions for salamanders. They

00:08:36.800 --> 00:08:40.480
spread down here from the Appalachians and quickly adapted to local

00:08:40.480 --> 00:08:46.950
environments. Today there are 150 species occupying different niches from the

00:08:46.950 --> 00:08:53.930
coast up to the mountaintops. 200 feet up in the canopy, bromeliads provide one

00:08:53.930 --> 00:09:00.000
species with a home. The palm salamander has to take great

00:09:00.000 --> 00:09:06.420
care not to fall out of its plant when hunting, but its semi-webbed feet give it a good grip,

00:09:06.420 --> 00:09:21.970
and it's got a particularly long tongue. There are so many different kinds of salamanders

00:09:21.970 --> 00:09:30.000
in the forests of Central America that every year another 20 or so new species are discovered.

00:09:30.000 --> 00:09:31.640
specialist lives in the soil.

00:09:32.430 --> 00:09:34.400
It's called Edipina, and it

00:09:34.400 --> 00:09:36.320
has adapted so well to its underground

00:09:36.320 --> 00:09:38.200
life that it actually looks

00:09:38.200 --> 00:09:54.030
like a worm. It's as long as

00:09:54.030 --> 00:09:56.270
your forearm, and it's become so

00:09:56.270 --> 00:09:58.300
worm-like that its tiny legs

00:09:58.300 --> 00:10:00.000
are no longer necessary to provide

00:10:00.000 --> 00:10:08.850
pellet. Indeed, they've almost disappeared altogether. This particular Edipina would be

00:10:08.850 --> 00:10:19.820
even longer had it not lost a piece of its tail. One of the strangest looking of all salamanders

00:10:19.820 --> 00:10:30.000
only emerges from the litter of the forest floor at night. It's the striated salamander. A

00:10:30.000 --> 00:10:36.820
leaves makes a good platform for which to catch flying insects and up here it's safer from snakes

00:10:36.820 --> 00:10:48.850
and other hunters of the forest floor its fully webbed feet act as suction cups and give it a

00:10:48.850 --> 00:10:49.770
secure hold

00:11:02.460 --> 00:11:10.080
In these tropical forests, the seasons are not well marked and all these various habitats are permanently moist.

00:11:10.080 --> 00:11:17.540
Some species that spend most of their time on land nevertheless need water in which to breed.

00:11:17.540 --> 00:11:25.830
In most North American swamp forests, rains can be relied upon every autumn.

00:11:25.830 --> 00:11:29.440
By early spring, these forests may be flooded.

00:11:30.160 --> 00:11:41.700
salamanders what could be better this female marble salamander chose her spot

00:11:41.700 --> 00:11:46.950
carefully she laid a hundred and fifty eggs at high watermark and has guarded

00:11:46.950 --> 00:11:54.870
them all winter she is adapted for life on land and breathe through lungs so now

00:11:54.870 --> 00:12:00.000
she must abandon her eggs or she will drown triggered by the rise

00:12:00.000 --> 00:12:14.470
water, the eggs start to hatch, and tadpoles with gills emerge. They feed on water fleas,

00:12:14.800 --> 00:12:23.560
but their first efforts are rather uncoordinated. They're totally defenceless,

00:12:23.920 --> 00:12:27.320
and there are plenty around who regard them as a good meal.

00:12:32.560 --> 00:12:35.350
Water beetles feast on them.

00:13:01.720 --> 00:13:08.000
This tadpole has perished, but out of its 149 brothers and sisters, there's a good chance

00:13:08.000 --> 00:13:21.670
that one or two will survive. For four months, marble tadpoles will feed and grow on the flooded

00:13:21.670 --> 00:13:30.000
forest floor. At first, they have gills with which to absorb oxygen from the water. Then, an extraordinary

00:13:30.000 --> 00:13:36.510
change takes place. As their lungs develop, they lose their gills and strengthen their

00:13:36.510 --> 00:13:49.620
legs and become very different-looking creatures. They still swim like fish, which is just as

00:13:49.620 --> 00:13:59.060
well. The water moccasin is a keen and swift hunter. Even a water spider could kill one.

00:14:01.880 --> 00:14:06.520
overcrowding in the lakes of a drying land was probably the impetus that drove

00:14:06.520 --> 00:14:11.700
salamanders out of water 65 million years ago and the water-living larvae of

00:14:11.700 --> 00:14:21.280
the marble salamander still repeat that evolutionary move every year on a dark

00:14:21.280 --> 00:14:30.000
wet night in April they all emerge at once 5,000 cautiously

00:14:30.090 --> 00:14:33.110
sniff the air, and run for cover in the wet leaves.

00:14:33.950 --> 00:14:36.990
For the next two years, they'll wander over the forest floor

00:14:36.990 --> 00:14:40.240
and perhaps find new ponds for themselves.

00:14:47.490 --> 00:14:54.310
They grow bigger and heavier over the summer

00:14:54.310 --> 00:14:56.670
and their warning colours develop.

00:15:04.540 --> 00:15:09.510
Fully grown and with poison in their skin to protect them, they're now relatively safe.

00:15:15.750 --> 00:15:22.460
A summer night in the Appalachians, and billions of red-backed salamanders are beginning new lives.

00:15:23.400 --> 00:15:28.110
They have completely broken the link with water and are totally terrestrial.

00:15:30.640 --> 00:15:34.980
In the cool dampness under every log and rock, there's a nursery.

00:15:38.610 --> 00:15:42.150
The female red-backed salamander protects her small brood.

00:15:42.770 --> 00:15:49.830
She too secretes a cocktail of antibiotics and fungicides to keep her eggs clean and healthy through their development.

00:15:49.830 --> 00:15:59.920
The half-formed embryos curled around their yolk sacs rotate very slowly within their trunk.

00:16:00.000 --> 00:16:29.920
transparent eggs. Blood vessels form, carry food from the yolk to the growing tissues.

00:16:30.760 --> 00:16:41.430
Limb buds appear and the eyes, vital for a life at night, grow large.

00:16:41.430 --> 00:16:51.640
Heart beats rhythmically and the stomach wall starts to thicken around the yolk sac.

00:16:51.640 --> 00:17:00.000
Chromatophores, the cells that will give color to the skin,

00:17:00.190 --> 00:17:05.420
begin to develop.

00:17:05.420 --> 00:17:23.820
After six weeks, the walls of the eggs at last begin to thin.

00:17:23.820 --> 00:17:29.220
These baby red-backed salamanders represent a crucial stage in evolutionary history.

00:17:30.410 --> 00:17:34.450
they have entirely missed out the aquatic tadpole stage.

00:17:34.450 --> 00:17:36.540
65 million years ago,

00:17:36.540 --> 00:17:38.940
it may well have been creatures like these

00:17:38.940 --> 00:17:45.400
that were the first backbone animals to reproduce on land.

00:17:45.400 --> 00:17:48.380
Even when they've emerged from their eggs,

00:17:48.380 --> 00:17:50.590
their stomachs are still full of yolk

00:17:50.590 --> 00:17:52.590
and they continue to grow.

00:17:52.590 --> 00:17:56.180
They will all live together as a family group for a year or so

00:17:56.180 --> 00:17:59.250
until they're large enough to defend themselves.

00:17:59.250 --> 00:18:00.000
In the meantime,

00:18:00.250 --> 00:18:10.380
mother will protect them from harm. Not all salamanders here are so independent of water.

00:18:11.080 --> 00:18:17.120
One, though it prefers land when adult, never strays far from the edge of streams, for water

00:18:17.120 --> 00:18:30.000
will be vital for her tadpoles. She's a dusky salamander, and she wears her eggs by

00:18:30.030 --> 00:18:36.400
proudly on her head. There are about twenty of them, glued together in a bunch. She will

00:18:36.400 --> 00:18:42.720
guard them for weeks, and throughout that time she won't feed, unable to discard her

00:18:42.720 --> 00:18:53.500
precious hat for even a moment. A seal salamander has the dusky's fresh eggs in his sights.

00:18:53.500 --> 00:18:58.980
The seal salamander is bigger and heavier, but the dusky's maternal instincts are strong,

00:18:58.980 --> 00:19:00.000
and she fights valiantly.

00:19:04.730 --> 00:19:26.380
She puts her own life at risk to protect her remaining eggs.

00:19:26.380 --> 00:19:30.000
The seal salamander is forced to retreat with only half

00:19:30.000 --> 00:19:38.570
the prize. Many eggs have been lost, but the mother dusky has done well to save a few from

00:19:38.570 --> 00:19:50.620
destruction. It's now autumn in the Appalachians, and down in the leaf litter, the red-legged

00:19:50.620 --> 00:19:52.960
salamanders are starting their courtship.

00:20:00.320 --> 00:20:11.240
night falls. For a salamander breeding on land, internal fertilization is essential.

00:20:11.240 --> 00:20:19.380
With his huge eyes and acute sense of smell, the male red-legged salamander soon fixes on the object of his affections.

00:20:19.380 --> 00:20:25.240
She is not to be one easily. He will have to display all his best features.

00:20:31.020 --> 00:20:39.320
"His lips anoint sexual stimulants on her skin, and so start the courtship."

00:20:39.320 --> 00:20:59.440
"Stimulated by his ointments and his waving red legs,

00:21:00.000 --> 00:21:30.000
She straddles his tail and joins in the dance around the floor for an hour or so.

00:21:30.440 --> 00:21:35.380
At the slightest hesitation, he will gently massage her chin with his tail

00:21:35.380 --> 00:21:39.220
until he has her full attention again.

00:22:10.840 --> 00:22:15.600
He stops on a patch of clean rock and deposits a spermatophore.

00:22:17.100 --> 00:22:20.670
A pillar of jelly topped with a packet of sperm.

00:22:21.910 --> 00:22:29.090
He walks forward and stops again.

00:22:30.000 --> 00:22:53.120
is now in exactly the right position to suck up the sperm into her body. It's all over. The pillar

00:22:53.120 --> 00:23:00.000
has lost its top, showing that the seduction was successful. But the male has invested a lot

00:23:00.000 --> 00:23:10.300
of time and effort in the proceedings, and he checks on its success.

00:23:10.300 --> 00:23:21.350
In South Carolina, the Marble Salamanders are waiting for their mating dance to begin.

00:23:21.350 --> 00:23:27.450
It's not going to be a private affair; thousands are expected to attend.

00:23:27.450 --> 00:23:30.000
The first big storm fixes the date.

00:23:30.320 --> 00:23:33.760
a huge amount of rain falls, soaking the forest floor

00:23:33.760 --> 00:23:37.960
and telling the marble salamanders that tonight is the night.

00:23:39.130 --> 00:23:42.430
Salamanders travel for as much as a kilometre from all directions

00:23:42.430 --> 00:23:43.910
to join in the party.

00:23:45.130 --> 00:23:48.010
Landmarks, smells and their internal compass

00:23:48.010 --> 00:23:51.460
help them to return to the place where they were hatched.

00:23:53.860 --> 00:24:00.000
The forest floor comes alive as 10,000

00:24:00.000 --> 00:24:12.240
salamanders join in the dance. Every female secretes a chemical signal from

00:24:12.240 --> 00:24:16.800
her skin that lets the males know that they found a female of the right species

00:24:16.800 --> 00:24:22.880
who's ready to mate. Everyone joins in.

00:24:58.120 --> 00:25:00.000
A male's reaction to these encounters

00:25:00.000 --> 00:25:02.440
is to put down a spermatophore.

00:25:09.270 --> 00:25:16.010
We can only hope that one of the females

00:25:16.010 --> 00:25:17.690
will come along and pick it up.

00:25:18.890 --> 00:25:20.930
There are many spermatophores to choose from,

00:25:21.350 --> 00:25:23.230
so to increase his own chances,

00:25:23.730 --> 00:25:26.230
every male deposits 20 or so.

00:25:33.930 --> 00:25:37.770
On another dark and stormy night in a few years' time,

00:25:38.220 --> 00:25:41.040
the offspring spawned during this dance

00:25:41.040 --> 00:25:43.300
will return to dance themselves,

00:25:43.860 --> 00:25:46.070
just as their ancestors have been doing

00:25:46.070 --> 00:25:49.840
for the last 65 million years.

